Executed grants and contracts scatter the terms needed for award setup across dozens of pages, exhibits, and amendments.
The Smith-Atlas Award Setup Assistant reads the fully executed agreement your team uploads (e.g., grant, cooperative agreement,
contract, subaward, or subcontract, plus any amendments) and extracts the terms needed for post-award administration into a
structured, traceable abstract spanning nine sections and 52 elements, from award numbers and project periods to F&A rates,
reporting requirements, intellectual property, and termination provisions.

Every extracted element carries a precise citation, including the source file, printed page, electronic page, and section
identifier, so a post-award research administrator or data entry specialist can navigate straight to the governing text and
verify it. When the agreement is silent on an element, it says so plainly with "N/A."

The system instructions are AI tool agnostic, so you can easily implement them into your preferred platform, including:

Gemini (Gems)
Claude (Projects)
ChatGPT (GPTs)
Microsoft Copilot (Agents)
Any in-house AI Assistants or Agents

Getting started requires minimal setup and zero coding experience. Simply copy and paste the system instructions (Loader.txt)
into your AI tool, then upload the accompanying process file (Process.txt) to its knowledge area.

What Your Team Gets in Every Extraction:

- A comprehensive nine-section, 52-element award abstract, built for setting up awards after execution.
- Precise citations for every answer, including the source file, printed page, electronic page, and section, to simplify verification.
- The conformed agreement: each element shows its current value first, followed by every amendment that changed it, so your team
can see the governing term and how it developed.
- Your choice of output: a Word document for review and manual setup, a system-neutral CSV file for importing into any research
administration system, or both from the same extraction.
- Answer-only values for identifiers, dates, and dollar amounts, with brief summaries of reporting, payment, and legal terms.

